<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <AssemblyName>LocalScribe.Inference</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>LocalScribe.Inference</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The opt-in remote inference helper (design 2026-10-18). One of the two projects in this
       solution permitted to reach the network (with LocalScribe.Fetch), and for the same reason it
       is a separate EXECUTABLE: the App starts it only for a job the user routed to a remote
       endpoint, so with remote inference off it never runs and no LocalScribe process that is
       running holds network code. Neither App nor Core references this project -
       NoNetworkInAppOrCoreTests pins that.

       DELIBERATELY has no ProjectReference to LocalScribe.Core either, the Fetch trade: the wire
       subset it speaks is written out by hand in HelperWire.cs, and InferenceHelperTests pins it
       against Core's AssistantWire in both directions. A Core reference would pull ONNX Runtime,
       OpenXml, NAudio and the Windows Desktop framework into the one process that talks to the
       network - measured at a 134 MB single-file exe, for a few JSON shapes.

       No PackageReference: HTTP, multipart and JSON all come from the base class library. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="LocalScribe.Core.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
